  "releaseNotes": "v9.3.3 - Playback timing (9.x legacy line):\n\nTwo fixes. No API removals; one new option, one new capability, one new event.\n\n- A stream that restarts late no longer stays late. The startup sync-error\n  baseline exists to absorb an output backend's first fill, and it was bounded\n  only by the 500 ms re-anchor threshold: a restart that landed half a second\n  late inside the re-anchor cooldown was absorbed as a \"constant offset that\n  will not be corrected\", and the stream played half a second late at a\n  reported error of zero for its whole duration (chrisuthe/windowsSpin#63).\n  The baseline is now bounded by\n  SyncCorrectionOptions.StartupBaselineAllowanceMicroseconds (150 ms); past\n  that the error stays visible and the one-shot snap tier closes it.\n- Live sources no longer cut out. A source role delivers audio in real time\n  from its first chunk, and the server schedules that chunk only min_buffer_ms\n  ahead; the buffer pre-rolls playback by the host's output latency, so a\n  WASAPI host's 100 ms prefill left about 50 ms of the default 150 ms for the\n  network and the decoder. required_lead_time_ms and min_buffer_ms are now\n  reported with the output latency added, from the initial client/state on:\n  ClientCapabilities.ExpectedOutputLatencyMs until a player has measured one,\n  the measured value after, re-reported through the new\n  IAudioPipeline.OutputLatencyChanged event only when the numbers move. Hosts\n  that had folded their backend's latency into RequiredLeadTimeMs or\n  MinBufferMs themselves should stop, or it is counted twice; WASAPI hosts\n  should set ExpectedOutputLatencyMs to their buffer size (typically 100 ms).\n\n----\n\n      Earlier release notes: https://github.com/Sendspin/sendspin-dotnet/releases",
